// Game: Postretro
// Format: Standard
// Switch-entity fixture. Sealed room, interior 448 x 320 x 160 units, wall
// thickness 16. One authored switch brush is mounted flush on the north wall:
// it is visible, solid, and pressable. Walk up to it, press use, and two things
// happen -- the console indicator light beside the switch fades up from dark,
// and the door slab in the west half drops. Activation is proximity-only with no
// facing check, so the indicator is the confirmation you can see while looking
// at the console; the door remains the independent signal several metres away.
// Manual gate for the switch compile-time desugar (static world geometry + a
// use-activation trigger whose every face grows up to use_reach, clamped to the
// free space in front of it) and for script animation of a dynamic-tier light.
// The console is flush on the north wall, so that face grows nothing -- which is
// the case the clamp exists to hold.
// The switch is fire_mode once, so the indicator lights once per level load and
// then stays lit. Reload to test again -- that is the fixture, not a bug.
// Compile: cargo run -p postretro-level-compiler -- content/dev/maps/switch-demo.map -o content/dev/maps/switch-demo.prl
// Run: cargo run -p xtask -- run content/dev/maps/switch-demo.prl
// Rebuild first: cargo build -p postretro-level-compiler --bin prl-build
// Stale binary compiles this map silently empty -- no geometry, no TriggerVolumes, no error.
// NOTE: keep map comments free of braces and quotes, and never leave a blank
// line inside an entity block -- the .map parser silently drops later entities.
// entity 0
{
"classname" "worldspawn"
"initialGravity" "-9.81"
"wad" ""
"data_script" "../scripts/switch-demo.ts"
"_tb_mod" "dev"
"_tb_def" "external:../../../sdk/TrenchBroom/postretro.fgd"
